s into serious  consideration and most importantly of  all at this Stan actually said it this  way i want to know that if i make a  decision today what's going to happen  six months from now am I going to be  regretting this decision we absolutely  have to take a long-term view we have to  think about where we're going to be in a  year or two years or three years I don't  think we would have gone into a rack if  we had seriously considered how it was  good how much it was going to benefit  Iran to have American armies take out  Saddam Hussein and if we had known how  much it was going to benefit on I don't  think we would have but I don't think  anybody looked at that because they were  all looking at short-term stuff they  were all ideologically motivated that is  the worst basis of foreign policy that  there could be looking down the road  where do you think things will net out  and Iraq and the Ukraine and I think  Iraq and Syria are finished as countries  this is where this is the remodeling of  the Middle East by forces in the Arab  world as opposed to the British and the  French who remodeled the Middle East  after World War one according to how  they wanted to draw the maps and so  that's going to happen and is it has  already essentially happened I think  Ukraine is going to be a long difficult  hall Putin as he has in georgia with the  taking of Abkhazia away from Georgia  will I I think try to continue to  destabilize Ukraine I think it's going  to be I hope these sanctions work and I  think they have a good  working but it'll be a long term  struggle not a short-term struggle we  had such I thought we had such a decent  relationship with Putin and all of a  sudden you know it turned why do you  think that happened I think you're going  to have to have somebody in this program  is an expert on Russia which I'm not and  Mike McFaul over whose book we're gonna  use a little later in the course is an  expert on Russia just seemed so peculiar  to me I don't think it's entirely / cute  peculiar I think first of all Putin's an  autocrat second all he's basically taken  away most of the democratic institutions  which were sprouting in Russia after the  collapse of the Soviet Union secondly I  think you have to culturally understand  sort of the paranoia of Russian Foreign  Policy's going back to Peter the Great  in order to sense thirdly you have to  understand the humiliation that the  Russians felt which is of course  encouraged by Putin for his own  political reasons after the collapse of  the Soviet Union they somehow field  entitled to own latvia estonia and  lithuania they don't have a democratic  tradition so they don't particularly  respect the right of the lithuanian the  Latvians and the Estonians to do what  they want and they think the same of the  Ukrainians and they have a peculiar view  of history which is work by their own  propaganda it just seemed for a while to  be moving in the right direction and  then it took a very wrong turn I think  if Medvedev was still president with any  power it would have taken going in the  right direction Putin is just the wrong  guy the wrong time remember he is a  trained KGB agent yes and I don't think  he's ever escaped that past and  unfortunately we're not now escaping it  either yeah unfortunately old habits die  hard I think okay final question I have  to ask I've read that you are interested  in running in 2016 for the president I  am NOT you're not no I'm supporting  Hillary Clinton uh okay well I had to  ask I think even though Hillary would be  an exception to this I usually you don't  go back a generation I think Obama  turned the page to a new generation I  think if Hillary doesn't run rather than  my running I think it should be somebody  like Kirsten Gillibrand or something  from a new generation but i do think  Hillary is the most qualified person in  America to be the president and I'm  happily supporting her very good thank  you so much for being here with 